  "answer": "This looks like a known limitation. The WSL2 localhost relay doesn't forward IPv4 127.0.0.1 to a dual-stack socket. Your `ss` output shows k3s listening on `*:6443`, which is such a socket. Windows then only gets the IPv6 `::1` forward, so `localhost` works and `127.0.0.1` fails. The archive tickets don't say why it works some of the time and not after a restart. The relay treats 127.0.0.1 and ::1 differently, and clients differ in which one `localhost` resolves to. Workarounds from those tickets: (1) Make the service listen on an IPv4 address, either 0.0.0.0 or 127.0.0.1. For k3s, set `bind-address: 0.0.0.0`, or the advertise or node IP, in /etc/rancher/k3s/config.yaml. (2) Point Windows tools such as Headlamp at `https://localhost:6443` or `https://[::1]:6443` instead of 127.0.0.1. Edit the server URL in the kubeconfig that Headlamp uses on Windows, and make sure 'localhost' is in the API server certificate's SANs (k3s `tls-san`). (3) Make hosts-file entries point to `::1` instead of 127.0.0.1. I found no fix for this in the archive."
